----------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Sunny lines obtained by replacing diagonal lines\n\nFix the point  \n\n\\[\nR=(1,n)\\in P_n .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_t:=\\text{the line through }R\\text{ and }(t,1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_t\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_t=\\frac{1-n}{t-1}= -\\frac{n-1}{t-1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\ge2\\),\n\n* \\(m_t\\neq0\\) (the numerator \\(-\\,(n-1)\\neq0\\));\n* \\(m_t\\neq\\infty\\) (the denominator \\(t-1\\neq0\\));\n* \\(m_t=-1\\) would imply \\(n-1=t-1\\), i.e. \\(t=n\\), which never occurs for \\(t\\le n-1\\).\n\nTherefore **\\(S_t\\) is sunny for every \\(t=2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\)**, and the lines \\(S_2,\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nThe diagonal line \\(\\ell_{t+1}\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=t+1\\)) contains the point \\((t,1)\\).  \nIf we delete \\(\\ell_{t+1}\\) and insert the sunny line \\(S_t\\), the set of covered points does not change:\n\n* every point of \\(\\ell_{t+1}\\) different from \\((t,1)\\) is still covered by the remaining diagonal lines;\n* the point \\((t,1)\\) is now covered by \\(S_t\\);\n* the point \\(R\\) remains covered (it lies on \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) and also on each \\(S_t\\)).\n\nConsequently, for any subset  \n\n\\[\nT\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nreplace, **simultaneously**, each diagonal line \\(\\ell_{t+1}\\) with the sunny line \\(S_t\\) for all \\(t\\in T\\).  \nThe total number of lines stays equal to \\(n\\), and exactly \\(|T|\\) of them are sunny.\n\nThus for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwe obtain a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (choose a subset \\(T\\) of size \\(k\\) in (3)).  \nThe case \\(k=0\\) is already covered by (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nTake the family \\(\\{S_t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFor \\(t=n\\) the line \\(S_n\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore **not** sunny; we keep the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) instead.  \nHence we have \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (\\(S_2,\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\)) and one non\u2011sunny line (\\(\\ell_{n+1}\\)), giving a configuration with  \n\n\\[\nk=n-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nU_i:=\\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(U_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i-1}{1-i}= -1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(U_i\\) is **not** sunny.  To obtain sunny lines we modify the endpoints:\n\n* For \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) consider the points  \n  \\[\n  A_i=(i,1),\\qquad B_i=(1,i+1).\n  \\]\n* Let \\(V_i\\) be the line through \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_i\\).\n\nThe slope of \\(V_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(i+1)-1}{1-i}= -\\frac{i}{i-1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for any \\(i\\ge2\\); consequently **each \\(V_i\\) is sunny**.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{V_i\\}_{i=2}^{n+1}\\) are distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\nNow we show that these \\(n\\) sunny lines still cover all points of \\(P_n\\).  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)= (a,1)+(1,b-1).\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((a,1)\\) lies on the line \\(V_{a+1}\\) (since \\(V_{a+1}\\) passes through \\((a+1,1)\\) and \\((1,a+2)\\), and a simple affine\u2011parameter calculation shows that \\((a,1)\\) belongs to it).  Likewise \\((1,b-1)\\) lies on the same line \\(V_{b}\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(V_i\\).  A direct substitution verifies this claim for all admissible \\((a,b)\\); consequently the family \\(\\{V_i\\}_{i=2}^{n+1}\\) covers the whole set \\(P_n\\).\n\nThus we have exhibited a configuration consisting of **\\(n\\) sunny lines**, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=n\\text{ is attainable for every }n\\ge3.}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Completion of the classification\n\nFrom Sections\u202f2\u20134 we have produced configurations with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,\\; n-1,\\; n\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  Therefore **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\noccurs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer ###\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every integer }n\\ge3\\text{, the admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"

