 and (5,1) are integer points.\n\nThus not triple.\n\nMaybe we need to use lines through (1,1) and other points: line through (1,1) and (2,2) slope = 1, allowed. That line also passes through (3,3) (since slope 1). Indeed points (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) are collinear on line y=x. But slope 1 is sunny (allowed). So we have triple (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). Good.\n\nNow remaining points after using that triple:\n\nUsed (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). Remove them.\n\nRemaining points:\n\n(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)\n(2,1),(2,4)\n(3,2)\n(4,1),(4,2)\n(5,1)\n\nTotal left = 12 - 3 = 9 points.\n\nWe need 3 more lines covering 9 points, average 3 per line.\n\nNow we have points left: Let's list them:\n\n(1,2)\n(1,3)\n(1,4)\n(2,1)\n(2,4)\n(3,2)\n(4,1)\n(4,2)\n(5,1)\n\nWe need to find triples.\n\nPotential triple: (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) not allowed. (1,2),(2,1),(3,0) not allowed.\n\nTry line through (1,2) and (2,4): slope = (4-2)/(2-1) = 2. Equation y = 2x + b => using (1,2): 2 = 2*1 + b => b = 0. So line y = 2x. Points on this line: (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nTry line through (1,2) and (3,2): slope = (2-2)/(3-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed.\n\nTry line through (1,2) and (4,2): slope = 0, not allowed.\n\nTry line through (1,2) and (5,1): slope = (1-2)/(5-1) = -1/4 = -0.25, allowed. Equation y = -0.25x + b => b = y + 0.25x = 2 + 0.25 = 2.25 = 9/4. So line y = -x/4 + 9/4. Check integer points: x=2 => y = -0.5 + 2.25 = 1.75 not integer; x=3 => y = -0.75 + 2.25 = 1.5; x=4 => y = -1 + 2.25 = 1.25; So only (1,2) and (5,1) are integer points.\n\nTry line through (1,3) and (2,1): slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2. Equation y = -2x + b => using (1,3): 3 = -2 + b => b = 5. So line y = -2x + 5. Points: (1,3),(2,1),(3,-1) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nTry line through (1,3) and (3,2): slope = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5. Equation y = -0.5x + b => using (1,3): 3 = -0.5 + b => b = 3.5 = 7/2. So line y = -x/2 + 7/2. Check x=2 => y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5; x=4 => y = -2 + 3.5 = 1.5; So only (1,3) and (3,2) are integer points.\n\nTry line through (1,3) and (4,1): slope = (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3 ≈ -0.6667. Equation y = -2/3 x + b => using (1,3): 3 = -2/3 + b => b = 3 + 2/3 = 11/3 ≈ 3.6667. Check x=2 => y = -4/3 + 11/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333; x=5 => y = -10/3 + 11/3 = 1/3 ≈ 0.333; So only (1,3) and (4,1) integer points.\n\nTry line through (1,3) and (5,1): slope = (1-3)/(5-1) = -2/4 = -0.5. Same as earlier line? Actually slope -0.5, line y = -0.5x + b => using (1,3): b = 3.5 = 7/2. So line y = -x/2 + 7/2, which includes (3,2) as we saw. So triple (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) are collinear? Let's check: (5,1) satisfies y = -0.5*5 + 3.5 = -2.5 + 3.5 = 1, yes! So points (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) are collinear on line y = -0.5x + 3.5 (i.e., y = -x/2 + 7/2). That line has slope -1/2, which is not prohibited. So we have a triple (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). Good.\n\nNow we have used (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). Remove them.\n\nRemaining points:\n\n(1,2)\n(1,4)\n(2,1)\n(2,4)\n(4,1)\n(4,2)\n\nTotal left = 6 points. Need 2 more lines covering 6 points, average 3 per line.\n\nNow we have points left:\n\n(1,2)\n(1,4)\n(2,1)\n(2,4)\n(4,1)\n(4,2)\n\nWe need to find two triples.\n\nPotential triple: (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) not allowed. (1,2),(2,1),(3,0) not allowed. (1,2),(4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333, line y = -x/3 + b => using (1,2): b = 2 + 1/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333. Check (2,1): y = -2/3 + 7/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not integer. (4,2): y = -4/3 + 7/3 = 3/3 = 1, not 2. So not triple.\n\nTry line through (1,4) and (2,1): slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, line y = -3x + b => using (1,4): b = 7. So line y = -3x + 7. Check (4,1): y = -12 + 7 = -5, not 1. So only two points.\n\nTry line through (1,4) and (4,2): slope = (2-4)/(4-1) = -2/3 ≈ -0.6667. Equation y = -2/3 x + b => using (1,4): b = 4 + 2/3 = 14/3 ≈ 4.667. Check (2,1): y = -4/3 + 14/3 = 10/3 ≈ 3.333, not 1. So no triple.\n\nTry line through (2,1) and (4,2): slope = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2 = 0.5. Equation y = 0.5x + b => using (2,1): 1 = 1 + b => b = 0. So line y = 0.5x. Points: (2,1),(4,2),(6,3) not in set. So only two points.\n\nTry line through (2,4) and (4,2): slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed (diagonal). So can't.\n\nTry line through (2,4) and (1,2): slope = (2-4)/(1-2) = -2/-1 = 2, line y = 2x + b => using (1,2): 2 = 2*1 + b => b = 0. So line y = 2x. Points: (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nTry line through (1,2) and (2,4) we already have line y = 2x, only those two.\n\nTry line through (1,2) and (4,2): slope = 0, horizontal, not allowed.\n\nTry line through (1,4) and (2,4): horizontal, not allowed.\n\nTry line through (2,1) and (4,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n\nThus we cannot find a triple among remaining points. However, we could consider using lines that cover 2 points each, but we have only 2 lines left, covering 6 points. If each line covers at most 3 points, we need each to cover exactly 3. But we can't find triples. Maybe we could have used a different selection of triples earlier to allow covering all points.\n\nLet's try to find a different set of triples. The problem is we need to partition the 12 remaining points into 4 triples. We found triples: (1,5),(2,3),(3,1) and (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) and (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). That's three triples, leaving 6 points that didn't form triples. Maybe we can find a different set of triples that covers all points. Let's attempt to find a covering with 5 lines, not necessarily partition into triples; lines can overlap. Overlap might allow covering leftover points with lines that also cover some already covered points, but that's okay. So we could use lines that cover 2 points each, but then we need more lines, but we only have 5 lines total. Overlap reduces coverage capacity. So we need to maximize coverage per line.\n\nWe could try to use lines that cover 4 points each. Is there a sunny line that contains 4 points in P_5? Let's search. For a line to contain 4 points, we need 4 points collinear with slope not 0,∞,-1. Let's try to find arithmetic progression in x and y coordinates that satisfy linear equation.\n\nGeneral line: y = m x + b, with rational m = p/q in lowest terms. For integer points (x_i, y_i) to lie on line, we need y_i = (p/q) x_i + b => q y_i = p x_i + q b. So q b must be integer for integer points. So line passes through integer lattice points if b rational with denominator dividing q.\n\nThus we need to find a line with slope p/q, intercept r/q (where r integer), such that many points (x,y) in P_5 satisfy q y = p x + r.\n\nWe can try to find lines with slope 1/2: equation 2y = x + r. For integer points, x + r must be even. Let's try r = 0: 2y = x => x even, y = x/2. Points: (2,1),(4,2). That's two points. r = 2: 2y = x + 2 => x even? Actually x+2 even => x even. Points: (2,2) => y=2, (4,3) => y=3 but (4,3) sum=7 >6 not allowed. So only (2,2). r = -2: 2y = x - 2 => x even, x≥2. Points: (2,0) not allowed, (4,1) allowed (y=1). So (4,1) only.\n\nSlope 2: equation y = 2x + b. For integer points, b integer. For x=1, y=2+b must be ≤5. So b can be -1 (y=1) => points (1,1),(2,3),(3,5) but (3,5) sum=8 >6 not allowed. So only (1,1),(2,3). b = -2 => (1,0) not allowed. b = 0 => (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) not allowed. So at most 2.\n\nSlope -2: y = -2x + b. For x=1, y = -2 + b ≤5 and positive. So b can be 3 (y=1), 4 (y=2), 5 (y=3), 6 (y=4), 7 (y=5). Let's test b=7: line y = -2x + 7 passes through (1,5),(2,3),(3,1) as we saw. That's three points. b=6: line y = -2x + 6 passes through (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not allowed. So only (1,4),(2,2). b=5: line y = -2x + 5 passes through (1,3),(2,1),(3,-1) not allowed. So two points. b=4: (1,2),(2,0) not allowed. So at most 3.\n\nSlope -3: y = -3x + b. For x=1, y = -3 + b ≤5, positive. b can be 4 (y=1),5 (y=2),6 (y=3),7 (y=4),8 (y=5). b=8: line y = -3x + 8 passes through (1,5),(2,2),(3,-1) not allowed. So only (1,5),(2,2). b=7: (1,4),(2,1),(3,-2) not allowed. So two points. b=6: (1,3),(2,0) not allowed. So at most 2.\n\nSlope -1/2: y = -0.5x + b. For integer points, need x even? Actually y = -x/2 + b => 2y = -x + 2b => x = 2b - 2y. So for each integer y, x determined. So points with integer x require 2b integer. Let's try b = 3.5 (7/2) as earlier line y = -x/2 + 7/2 passes through (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) three points. That's triple we used. Could there be a line with 4 points? Let's try b = 5 (b=5). Then line y = -x/2 + 5 => 2y = -x + 10 => x = 10 - 2y. For y integer, x integer. Points: y=1 => x=8 (not in range), y=2 => x=6 (not in range), y=3 => x=4 (allowed: (4,3) sum=7 >6 not allowed), y=4 => x=2 (allowed: (2,4) sum=6 allowed), y=5 => x=0 not allowed. So only (2,4) maybe. So not many.\n\nSlope 1/3: y = x/3 + b. For integer points, need x divisible by 3? Actually 3y = x + 3b => x = 3y - 3b. For integer x, need 3b integer. Let's try b = 1: line y = x/3 + 1 => 3y = x + 3 => x = 3y - 3. For y=1 => x=0 not allowed. y=2 => x=3, (3,2) allowed. y=3 => x=6 not allowed. So only (3,2). So not many.\n\nThus max points per sunny line for n=5 seems to be 3. So covering 15 points with 5 lines each covering at most 3 points is possible only if each line covers exactly 3 points and they are disjoint (no overlap). We found three triples, but we need two more triples covering remaining six points. We attempted to find triples among remaining points but didn't find any. Perhaps there is a different selection of triples that covers all points. Let's search systematically for triples in P_5.\n\nWe can generate all triples of points that are collinear with slope not 0,∞,-1. Let's attempt to find all possible triples.\n\nWe have points in P_5 as above. Let's list them again:\n\n(1,1)\n(1,2)\n(1,3)\n(1,4)\n(1,5)\n(2,1)\n(2,2)\n(2,3)\n(2,4)\n(3,1)\n(3,2)\n(3,3)\n(4,1)\n(4,2)\n(5,1)\n\nWe need to find all collinear triples with slope not 0,∞,-1.\n\nWe can systematically search for lines determined by any two points, then check if a third point lies on same line.\n\nWe can compute slopes for each pair and see if any third point matches.\n\nBut we can also think of lines of the form y = m x + b with rational m, b.\n\nLet's enumer