 to cover each hypotenuse point individually, and the remaining one sunny line to cover other interior points. That seems plausible: we can assign each hypotenuse point to its own sunny line, each line also passes through a point on y=1 (to cover row points). Since each line must intersect y=1 at a distinct point, we can match each hypotenuse point (a,b) with a distinct a_i (the x-coordinate of the row point). So we need a mapping from the n-2 hypotenuse points to n-1 row points (a_i,1) (a_i from 2..n). Since there are n-2 hypotenuse points, we have enough row points (n-1) to assign each hypotenuse point a distinct row point, leaving one row point unused (or used for covering other interior points). So we can construct n-2 lines each passing through a hypotenuse point (a,b) and a row point (c,1) where c != a (to avoid slope -1). Then we need to ensure the line is sunny (slope not -1). The slope of line through (a,b) and (c,1) is (1-b)/(c-a). Since b = n+1-a, we have 1 - (n+1-a) = a - n. So slope = (a-n)/(c-a). For slope to be -1, we need a-n = -(c-a) => a-n = a-c => -n = -c => c = n. So the line will have slope -1 only if c = n. So as long as we avoid using c = n for the line with a given a, we avoid slope -1. So we can assign each hypotenuse point (a,b) to a row point (c,1) where c != a and c != n? Actually we just need to avoid c = n for the line that would have slope -1? Let's check: slope = (a-n)/(c-a). If c = n, slope = (a-n)/(n-a) = -1 (since numerator = -(n-a), denominator = n-a). So indeed, if we pair a hypotenuse point with row point (n,1), the line will have slope -1, which is not allowed. So we must avoid using (n,1) as the row point for any hypotenuse point. But we have n-1 row points (2..n). So we can avoid using (n,1) for the n-2 hypotenuse points, leaving (n,1) for the extra line that covers other interior points. That seems plausible.\n\nThus a construction for k=n-1 could be: Use vertical line x=1 (non-sunny). For each a from 2 to n-1 (i.e., n-2 val