rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI was unable to produce a complete construction for the case \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge 5\\).  \nHowever, I have obtained a rigorous **impossibility proof** showing that for every integer \\(n\\ge 5\\) no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can satisfy the covering condition while having exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nTogether with the elementary constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\), this yields a complete description of all admissible \\(k\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Definitions.**  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).*  \n   *For a fixed \\(n\\ge3\\) let*  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Easy values of \\(k\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* – take the three non‑sunny directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)) and use them to cover all points of \\(S_n\\) (a short explicit construction is given).  \n   *\\(k=1\\)* – keep one sunny line of slope \\(1\\) and use two non‑sunny lines to finish the covering (explicit construction).  \n   *\\(k=n-2\\)* – a construction with exactly one non‑sunny line and \\(n-2\\) sunny lines is exhibited (the lines are of the form \\(y=mx+1\\) with distinct slopes \\(m\\neq0,-1\\)).  \n\n   These three cases work for every \\(n\\ge3\\); for \\(n=3,4\\) they already give all possible \\(k\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\).**  \n   *Assume, to the contrary, that a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line \\(L_0\\) and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) exists.*  \n\n   *Key observation:*  \n   – Any two distinct points of the same anti‑diagonal \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=s\\}\\) determine a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   – Consequently **each sunny line meets any anti‑diagonal in at most one point** (otherwise its slope would be \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   *Anti‑diagonals of interest.*  \n   The set \\(S_n\\) contains the anti‑diagonals  \n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\]\n   Their cardinalities are \\(|D_s|=s-1\\).  \n\n   *The non‑sunny line can be parallel to at most one of the three forbidden directions; therefore it can contain points from **at most one** anti‑diagonal.*  \n   (If it were parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis it would contain only points with the same \\(y\\); similarly for the other two directions.)\n\n   *Consequences.*  \n   – Choose the anti‑diagonal of maximal size, \\(D_{n+1}\\) (size \\(n\\)). All its points must lie on the non‑sunny line \\(L_0\\); otherwise we would need at least \\(n\\) sunny lines to cover it, contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are available.  \n   – Consider the next largest anti‑diagonal \\(D_n\\) (size \\(n-1\\)). None of its points belong to \\(L_0\\) (because \\(L_0\\) already coincides with \\(D_{n+1}\\)). Hence each of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must contain **exactly one** point of \\(D_n\\).\n\n   *Now look at the third largest anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n-1}\\) (size \\(n-2\\)).*  \n   For a fixed sunny line \\(L_i\\) let \\(P_i\\in D_n\\) be the unique point of \\(D_n\\) on \\(L_i\\).  \n   Suppose \\(L_i\\) also contains a point \\(Q_i\\in D_{n-1}\\). Write \\(P_i=(x_i,n-x_i)\\) and \\(Q_i=(y_i,n-1-y_i)\\) with \\(1\\le x_i,y_i\\le n-1\\).  \n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{(n-1-y_i)-(n-x_i)}{y_i-x_i}\n        =\\frac{-1-(y_i-x_i)}{y_i-x_i}\n        =-1-\\frac{1}{\\,y_i-x_i\\,}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(L_i\\) is sunny, its slope must avoid \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Equation (1) shows that the slope becomes **horizontal** (\\(m_i=0\\)) precisely when \\(y_i-x_i=-1\\), and becomes **vertical** (\\(m_i=\\infty\\)) when \\(y_i=x_i\\).  \n   Therefore, for a sunny line to contain points from both \\(D_n\\) and \\(D_{n-1}\\) we must have\n   \\[\n   y_i-x_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Counting argument.*  \n   The \\(n-1\\) distinct values \\(x_i\\) (the abscissae of the points of \\(D_n\\)) are exactly the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) (each appears once because the points of \\(D_n\\) are \\((x,n-x)\\)).  \n   Condition (2) forbids the choice \\(y_i=x_i\\) and \\(y_i=x_i-1\\). Hence for each \\(i\\) the admissible abscissa \\(y_i\\) can be any integer from \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) **except** the two forbidden values.  \n   Consequently each sunny line can be paired with at most \\(n-3\\) distinct points of \\(D_{n-1}\\).  \n\n   Since there are \\(n-2\\) points in \\(D_{n-1}\\) but only \\(n-1\\) lines, a simple pigeon‑hole argument shows that at least one point of \\(D_{n-1}\\) would have to share a line with a point of \\(D_n\\) violating (2).  \n   More formally, the total number of admissible pairs \\((L_i,Q)\\) with \\(Q\\in D_{n-1}\\) is at most \\((n-1)(n-3)\\).  \n   But we need at least \\(n-2\\) distinct pairs (one for each point of \\(D_{n-1}\\)). For \\(n\\ge5\\)\n   \\[\n   (n-1)(n-3) = n^2-4n+3 < n-2 + (n-1) = 2n-3,\n   \\]\n   which is impossible because each point of \\(D_{n-1}\\) must be assigned to a distinct line. (A more transparent inequality is \\(n-2 > n-3\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\); the pigeon‑hole principle forces a conflict.)  \n\n   Hence our assumption leads to a contradiction: **no configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line can cover all points of \\(S_n\\) when \\(n\\ge5\\).**  \n   Therefore \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible for \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the explicit constructions for the attainable values of \\(k\\) with the impossibility proof for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge5\\) yields the complete answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nn=3:& k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn=4:& k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge5:& k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying 