-2\\)).  \n   Its slope is\n   \\[\n   m_{i,j}= \\frac{(n-1-j)-(n-i)}{j-i}\n          =\\frac{-1-(j-i)}{j-i}\n          =-1-\\frac{1}{\\,j-i\\,}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The line is sunny exactly when \\(j-i\\neq0,-1\\); the two forbidden values would give slopes \\(-1\\) and \\(0\\), respectively.\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 a bipartite\u2011matching formulation.*  \n   Consider the bipartite graph \\(G\\) whose left part consists of the indices \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) (the sunny lines) and whose right part consists of the indices \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) (the points of \\(D_{n-1}\\)).  \n   We join \\(i\\) to \\(j\\) by an edge iff \\(j-i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\); by (1) this is precisely the condition that the line through \\(P_i\\) and the point of \\(D_{n-1}\\) with abscissa \\(j\\) is sunny.\n\n   To cover all points of \\(D_{n-1}\\) we would need a matching that saturates the right part (each point of \\(D_{n-1}\\) must be assigned to a distinct sunny line).  \n   Hall\u2019s marriage theorem says that such a matching exists iff for every subset \\(I\\) of the left vertices,\n   \\[\n   |N(I)|\\ge |I|,\n   \\]\n   where \\(N(I)\\) is the set of neighbours of \\(I\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f5 \u2013 Hall\u2019s condition fails for \\(n\\ge5\\).*  \n   Take the subset\n   \\[\n   I=\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\}.\n   \\]\n   For each \\(i\\in I\\) the forbidden neighbours are \\(j=i\\) and \\(j=i-1\\).  \n   Hence the set of neighbours of \\(I\\) is contained in\n   \\[\n   \\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\setminus\\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\}\n                                 \\cup\\{0,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor-1\\}\\bigr)\n   =\\{\\,\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor+1,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   |N(I)|\\le n-2-\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+1\n          = n-2-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   While\n   \\[\n   |I|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   For \\(n\\ge5\\) we have\n   \\[\n   |N(I)|=n-2-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n          <\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor =|I|,\n   \\]\n   violating Hall\u2019s condition.  \n   Therefore no assignment of the points of \\(D_{n-1}\\) to the sunny lines is possible, contradicting the assumption that a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line exists.\n\n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge5\\) a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines is impossible.\n\n4. **Collecting the answers.**  \n\n   * For \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\) a direct inspection of the constructions above shows that the only attainable values are\n     \\[\n     k\\in\\{0,1,\\,n-2\\}=\\{0,1,1\\}\\text{ for }n=3,\\qquad\n     k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\text{ for }n=4 .\n     \\]\n   * For every \\(n\\ge5\\) the constructions give admissible \\(k\\) for all\n     \\[\n     0\\le k\\le n-2,\n     \\]\n     and the impossibility proof rules out \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n   Consequently the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nn=3:& k\\in\\{0,1,1\\}=\\{0,1\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn=4:& k\\in\\{0,1,2\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge5:& k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\n(For \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=2\\) coincides with \\(n-1\\) and is impossible; the only possible values are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).)"

