3,2),(5,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered points: from previous lines: L1: y=x (covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3)). L2: y = -2x + 7 (covers (1,5),(2,3),(3,1)). L3: y = -0.5 x + 3.5 (covers (1,3),(3,2),(5,1)). So far covered points: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,5),(2,3),(3,1),(1,3),(3,2),(5,1). That's 9 points.\n\nRemaining points: (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(3,? ) (3,? ) we have (3,? ) left: (3,? ) we have (3,? ) points: (3,1) already covered, (3,2) covered, (3,3) covered. So none left in row a=3. Row a=4: (4,1),(4,2). Row a=5: (5,1) covered. Row a=2: (2,1),(2,4). Row a=1: (1,2),(1,4). So remaining points: (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2). That's 6 points.\n\nWe need 2 more sunny lines (since we have used 3 lines, need total 5). Each line can cover at most 3 points. So we need to partition these 6 points into 2 sunny lines each covering exactly 3 points.\n\nLet's see if we can find a sunny line covering (1,2),(2,4),(4,2) maybe? Let's test line through (1,2) and (2,4): slope (4-2)/(2-1) = 2, sunny. Equation: y = 2x + b. Plug (1,2): 2 = 2*1 + b => b = 0. So line y = 2x. Check (4,8) not allowed. (3,6) not allowed. So line only covers (1,2),(2,4). That's 2 points.\n\nTry line through (1,2) and (4,1): slope (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333..., sunny. Equation: y = -(1/3)x + b. Plug (1,2): 2 = -(1/3) + b => b = 2 + 1/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333. So line y = -(1/3)x + 7/3. Check (2,? ) => y = -(2/3) + 7/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.666, not integer. (3,? ) => y = -(1) + 7/3 = 4/3 ≈ 1.333, not integer. (4,1) works. So line only covers (1,2),(4,1). That's 2 points.\n\nTry line through (1,2) and (4,2): slope (2-2)/(4-1) = 0, horizontal, forbidden.\n\nTry line through (1,2) and (2,1): slope (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, forbidden.\n\nTry line through (1,2) and (5,1): (5,1) already covered, but slope (1-2)/(5-1) = -1/4 = -0.25, sunny. Equation: y = -0.25x + b. Plug (1,2): 2 = -0.25 + b => b = 2.25 = 9/4. Check (2,? ) => y = -0.5 + 9/4 = 7/4 = 1.75, not integer. (3,? ) => y = -0.75 + 9/4 = 9/4 - 3/4 = 6/4 = 1.5, not integer. So only (1,2),(5