ints with c=-3 or -4. So we need more lines.\n\nThus using only n lines of slope 1 cannot cover all points. So the construction fails.\n\nThus we need to see if any other construction can achieve k=n for odd n. Let's think: For odd n, we need to cover N points with n lines, each covering at most M_n = (n+1)/2 points (since n odd => (n+1)/2 integer). So each line must cover exactly M_n points. For n=5, M_n = 3. So each line must cover exactly 3 points. So we need a partition of the points into 5 triples, each collinear on a sunny line. We attempted to find such a partition for n=5 but didn't succeed yet. Let's try more systematically.\n\nWe need to find 5 lines each covering exactly 3 points. The lines must be distinct (since we need n distinct lines). They must be sunny (slope not 0,∞,-1). Let's search for all possible triples in S_5.\n\nWe can enumerate triples manually or think about geometry.\n\nThe set S_5 is a triangular shape with rows:\n\nRow a=1: b=1..5 (5 points)\na=2: b=1..4 (4 points)\na=3: b=1..3 (3 points)\na=4: b=1..2 (2 points)\na=5: b=1 (1 point)\n\nCoordinates:\n\n(1,1)\n(1,2)\n(1,3)\n(1,4)\n(1,5)\n\n(2,1)\n(2,2)\n(2,3)\n(2,4)\n\n(3,1)\n(3,2)\n(3,3)\n\n(4,1)\n(4,2)\n\n(5,1)\n\nWe already have triple (1,1)-(2,2)-(3,3) slope 1.\n\nTriple (1,5)-(2,3)-(3,1) slope -2.\n\nNow we need 3 more triples covering remaining 9 points.\n\nRemaining points: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1).\n\nWe need to find triples among these.\n\nLet's list all possible triples among these points that are collinear with a sunny slope.\n\nWe can try to find lines through (1,2) and (2,4): slope (4-2)/(2-1)=2, line y = 2x? Actually through (1,2): 2 = 2*1 + c => c=0, so line y = 2x. Check (3,6) not in set. So only two points.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (3,2): slope (2-2)/(3-1)=0, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (4,1): slope (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, which is not -1, not 0, not infinite, so sunny. Equation: y-2 = -1/3 (x-1) => y = -1/3 x + 7/3. Check if any other