hese.\n\nRemaining points: (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2).\n\nLet's see if we can find a triple with slope something.\n\nConsider line through (1,2) and (2,4): slope 2, line y = 2x? Actually through (1,2): 2 = 2*1 + c => c=0, so y=2x. Check (4,8) not in set. So only two.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (4,1): slope (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, we saw only two.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (4,2): slope (2-2)/(4-1)=0, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (2,1): slope (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed (non-sunny). So can't.\n\nLine through (1,4) and (2,1): slope (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, sunny. Equation: y = -3x + c; plugging (1,4): 4 = -3 + c => c = 7. So line y = -3x + 7. Check (4, -5) not. So only two.\n\nLine through (1,4) and (4,1): slope (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,4) and (4,2): slope (2-4)/(4-1) = -2/3, sunny. Equation: y = -2/3 x + c; plugging (1,4): 4 = -2/3 + c => c = 4 + 2/3 = 14/3. Check (2,? ) y = -2/3*2 + 14/3 = -4/3 + 14/3 = 10/3 ≈ 3.333, not integer. (3,? ) y = -2 + 14/3 = 8/3 ≈ 2.667. So only two.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (4,2): slope (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2, sunny. Equation: y = (1/2)x + c; plugging (2,1): 1 = 1 + c => c = 0. So line y = x/2. Check (1,0.5) not integer. (3,1.5) not integer. So only (2,1) and (4,2) are integer points.\n\nLine through (2,4) and (4,1): slope (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2, sunny. Equation: y = -3/2 x + c; plugging (2,4): 4 = -3 + c => c = 7. So line y = -3/2 x + 7. Check (4,1) works: y = -3/2*4 + 7 = -6 + 7 = 1. Good. Check (1,? ) y = -3/2 + 7 = 11/2 = 5.5 not integer. So only two.\n\nLine through (2,4) and (1,2): slope 2, we already considered.\n\nLine through (2,4) and (4,2): slope (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,4) and (2,1): vertical line x=2, not allowed.\n\nLine through (4,1) and (4,2): vertical line x=4, not allowed.\n\nThus no triple among these 6 points? Let's try to find a triple using (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) not in set. (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not. (1,4),(2,2) slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, line y = -2x + 6, check (3,0) not. So only two.\n\n(1,2),(2,4),(3,6) not.\n\n(1,2),(2,1) slope -1 not allowed.\n\n(1,4),(2,1) slope -3, line y = -3x + 7, check (3, -2) not.\n\n(1,4),(2,4) horizontal not allowed.\n\n(1,2),(2,1) slope -1 not allowed.\n\n(1,2),(4,1) slope -1/3, we saw only two.\n\n(1,4),(4,2) slope -2/3, only two.\n\n(2,1),(4,2) slope 1/2, only two.\n\n(2,4),(4,1) slope -3/2, only two.\n\n(2,4),(4,2) slope -1, not allowed.\n\n(1,2),(4,2) horizontal not allowed.\n\n(1,4),(2,1) slope -3, only two.\n\n(2,1),(1,4) slope -3, only two.\n\nThus seems no triple among remaining points. So we cannot partition the remaining 6 points into two triples. So our earlier selection of triples may be suboptimal. Perhaps we need a different set of triples.\n\nLet's search for a different partition.\n\nWe have found three triples: (1,1)-(2,2)-(3,3) (slope 1), (1,5)-(2,3)-(3,1) (slope -2), (1,3)-(3,2)-(5,1) (slope -1/2). That's 9 points covered. Remaining points: (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2). Same as before. So we need to cover these 6 points with two triples.\n\nLet's see if we can find a triple among these 6 points that we missed. Perhaps there is a triple using points not all from the same set we considered earlier. Let's list all possible triples among these six points.\n\nSet A = { (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2) }.\n\nWe need to find collinear triples with slope not 0,∞,-1.\n\nWe can try to compute slopes for each pair and see if a third point lies on same line.\n\nList all pairs and slopes:\n\n- (1,2)-(1,4): vertical (x=1) not allowed.\n- (1,2)-(2,1): slope (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed.\n- (1,2)-(2,4): slope (4-2)/(2-1) = 2, allowed.\n- (1,2)-(4,1): slope (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, allowed.\n- (1,2)-(4,2): slope (2-2)/(4-1) = 0, not allowed.\n\n- (1,4)-(2,1): slope (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, allowed.\n- (1,4)-(2,4): horizontal, not allowed.\n- (1,4)-(4,1): slope (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed.\n- (1,4)-(4,2): slope (2-4)/(4-1) = -2/3, allowed.\n\n- (2,1)-(2,4): vertical, not allowed.\n- (2,1)-(4,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n- (2,1)-(4,2): slope (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2, allowed.\n\n- (2,4)-(4,1): slope (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2, allowed.\n- (2,4)-(4,2): slope (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\n- (4,1)-(4,2): vertical, not allowed.\n\nNow for each allowed slope, check if a third point lies on same line.\n\nLet's examine slope 2 line through (1,2) and (2,4). Equation: y = 2x? Actually through (1,2): 2 = 2*1 + c => c = 0, so y = 2x. Check if any other point in set A lies on y=2x: (4,8) not; (3,6) not; (4,1) no; (2,1) no; (1,4) no; (4,2) no. So only two.\n\nSlope -1/3 line through (1,2) and (4,1). Equation: y = -1/3 x + b; using (1,2): 2 = -1/3 + b => b = 7/3. So line y = -1/3 x + 7/3. Check other points: (2,? ) y = -2/3 + 7/3 = 5/3 ≈1.667 not integer; (3,? ) y = -1 + 7/3 = 4/3 ≈1.333; (4,1) is on line; (2,4) not; (1,4) not; (4,2) not. So only two.\n\nSlope -3 line through (1,4) and (2,1). Equation: y = -3x + b; using (1,4): 4 = -3 + b => b = 7. So y = -3x + 7. Check (4,? ) y = -12 + 7 = -5 not; (3,? ) y = -9 + 7 = -2; (4,1) not; (4,2) not; (2,4) not; (1,2) not. So only two.\n\nSlope -2/3 line through (1,4) and (4,2). Equation: y = -2/3 x + b; using (1,4): 4 = -2/3 + b => b = 4 + 2/3 = 14/3. So y = -2/3 x + 14/3. Check (2,? ) y = -4/3 + 14/3 = 10/3 ≈3.333; (3,? ) y = -2 + 14/3 = 8/3 ≈2.667; (4,2) is on line; (1,2) not; (2,1) not; (2,4) not; (4,1) not. So only two.\n\nSlope 1/2 line through (2,1) and (4,2). Equation: y = (1/2)x + c; using (2,1): 1 = 1 + c => c = 0, so y = x/2. Check (1,0.5) not integer; (3,1.5) not; (4,2) is on line; (2,4) not; (1,2) not; (1,4) not; (4,1) not. So only two.\n\nSlope -3/2 line through (2,4) and (4,1). Equation: y = -3/2 x + b; using (2,4): 4 = -3 + b => b = 7. So y = -3/2 x + 7. Check (1,? ) y = -1.5 + 7 = 5.5 not integer; (3,? ) y = -4.5 + 7 = 2.5 not integer; (4,1) is on line; (2,1) not; (1,2) not; (1,4) not; (4,2) not. So only two.\n\nThus no triple among these six points. So our selection of triples fails to cover all points w