at diagonal. But a sunny line can contain at most floor((n+1)/2) points, which is less than n (the size of a diagonal). So we cannot cover an entire diagonal with a single sunny line. So we need to replace multiple diagonals with multiple sunny lines, each covering some points of each replaced diagonal. The solution attempted to replace each diagonal ℓ_t with a sunny line that covers two points of that diagonal (including (1,1)). But that leaves many points uncovered. So we need a more clever scheme.\n\nPerhaps we can use a set of sunny lines that collectively cover all points, while using some diagonals to cover the rest. The problem is reminiscent of covering a triangular grid with lines of three directions (horizontal, vertical, diagonal) and some other slopes. The non-sunny lines are limited to three directions. The sunny lines can be any other direction. We need to choose k sunny lines and n-k non-sunny lines (which can be any of the three directions). The non-sunny lines can be horizontal, vertical, or diagonal. The solution used only diagonal lines for non-sunny lines. But we could also use horizontal and vertical lines.\n\nThus perhaps we can construct for any k by using k sunny lines each covering floor((n+1)/2) points, and the remaining points covered by n-k non-sunny lines (horizontal, vertical, diagonal). We need to ensure we have enough capacity.\n\nWe have N total points. Sunny lines cover at most k M_n points. Non-sunny lines each can cover at most n points. So total capacity is k M_n + (n-k)n. The bound we derived is exactly the capacity bound. If we can achieve equality, we can cover all points. For each k <= bound, we need to find a covering that uses exactly k sunny lines and n-k non-sunny lines, achieving capacity N. The bound is derived from capacity; we need to achieve it.\n\nThus we need to find a covering that saturates the capacity bound for each k. For k = 0, we have capacity = n*n = n^2, but N = n(n+1)/2, which is less than n^2, so we have