=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Maximum points on a sunny line (Lemma 1).**  \n   If a line is sunny (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) then it contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M_n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Necessary inequality (Lemma 2).**  \n   If exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then  \n\n   \\[\n   N\\le kM_n+(n-k)n .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(N\\) and \\(M_n\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1 & (n\\text{ even}),\\\\[1mm]\n   n   & (n\\text{ odd}).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{∗}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) (Lemma 3).**  \n   For odd \\(n=2t+1\\;(t\\ge2)\\) we have \\(M_n=t+1\\).  \n   If \\(k=n\\) then (∗) forces every sunny line to contain exactly \\(M_n\\) points, i.e. three points.  \n   A sunny line can contain three points of \\(S_n\\) only when its slope is \\(1\\) and its equation is \\(y=x+d\\) with \\(d\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).  \n   Consequently at most two sunny lines can contain three points; the remaining \\(n-2\\) sunny lines would contain at most two points each, contradicting the equality \\(N=kM_n\\).  \n   Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible for odd \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n5. **Sufficiency (Lemma 4).**  \n   For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we construct \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Base families.*  \n   The \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   are all non‑sunny and together cover \\(S_n\\); thus \\(k=0\\) is always possible.\n\n   *Increasing the number of sunny lines.*  \n   Choose any integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \n   On the diagonal \\(D_c\\) pick the point \\(P_c=(1,c-1)\\).  \n   Through \\(P_c\\) draw a sunny line with irrational slope \\(\\alpha_c\\) (for instance \\(\\alpha_c=\\sqrt{2}+c\\)).  \n   Because \\(\\alpha_c\\) is irrational, the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\;y-\\,(c-1)=\\alpha_c\\,(x-1)\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   contains **exactly one** point of \\(S_n\\), namely \\(P_c\\); it contains no other lattice point of the triangle.  \n   The line \\(L_c\\) is sunny (its slope is neither \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) and it is distinct from all other lines constructed in this way because the chosen points are different.\n\n   Replace the diagonal \\(D_c\\) in the family (1) by the sunny line \\(L_c\\).  \n   The new family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and still covers all points of \\(S_n\\): the only point of \\(D_c\\) that is lost is \\(P_c\\), but this point is now covered by \\(L_c\\).\n\n   Performing the above replacement for **any** set of \\(k\\) distinct diagonals produces a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Since we may choose the diagonals arbitrarily, every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\) for even \\(n\\) and \\(k\\le n\\) for odd \\(n\\).  \n   Lemma 3 shows that the case \\(k=n\\) never occurs when \\(n\\) is odd and \\(n\\ge5\\).  \n   Lemma 4 exhibits explicit constructions for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n   \\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3 .\n   \\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 